rules/schrybwys.md and rules/schrybwys-compact.md
-------------------------------------------------
Licensed CC BY-SA 4.0; see rules/LICENSE. These describe the codified "schriftsprach-nah" spelling system for Bernese
German. That system is not ours. It was established by:

  Werner Marti, "Bärndütschi Schrybwys" (Francke, Bern, 1978) and
  "Berndeutsch-Grammatik" (Francke, Bern, 1985)

  Otto von Greyerz / Ruth Bietenhard, "Berndeutsches Wörterbuch"
  (10th edition, Cosmos, 2017)

and is summarised for the public by berndeutsch.ch, notably in:

  Ursula Pinheiro-Weber, "Bärndütsch schrybe: schriftsprach-nach" (2021),
  licensed CC BY-ND 4.0
  https://www.berndeutsch.ch/doc/berndeutsch-schreiben-schriftsprach-nah-v1.pdf

  Hans Jürg Zingg, "Bärndütsch schrybe: uussprach-nach" (2021), the
  aussprach-nah counterpart, same licence
  https://www.berndeutsch.ch/doc/berndeutsch-schreiben-aussprach-nah-v1.pdf

  https://www.berndeutsch.ch/pages/schrybe (the free summary page)

The rules a spelling system prescribes are facts about that system, and they
are stated here in our own words and our own arrangement.

About the examples, measured rather than asserted. An earlier version of this
file named five words as shared with the Pinheiro-Weber PDF. A reviewer checked
and three of them (Gipfeli, Meiteli, Spiez) are not in that document at all;
they come from the free berndeutsch.ch summary page. The claim was wrong in
both directions, so here is the actual count.

rules/schrybwys.md uses 176 distinct illustrative words. 84 of them, about 47%,
also occur in the PDF: among others vergässe, Fride, sibe, ligt, Fröid, Höi,
nöi, tröime, fahre, meh, gseh, näh, gah, verstah, blüeje, Chräje, Poscht,
luschtig, Wäschpi, Stei, spile, starch, verstecke, umespringe, bruucht, trunke,
kämpft, ggange, ddänkt, bbout, Sache, Loch, Unggle, Stäcke, hetze, locker,
Hang, angers, mitenang, wi ne Bär, Handy, Klinik, Pandemie, Saal, Boot, Aabe,
nüüt, alli, aui, wölle, wöue, dihr, heit, chömet.

That is a lot, and pretending otherwise would be silly. These are the standard
illustrations for these rules; for several rules they are close to the only
ones that exist, and we learned the rules from that document. The other 92
words are our own, chosen freely and verified against a berndeutsch.ch
dictionary entry with scripts/bdw.

What is ours is the prose and the arrangement. No sentence from either CC BY-ND
document is copied. The rulebook opens with a section that document does not
have ("What gives a text away"), and the consonant section is ordered by what
each phenomenon is (sound changes, then the joint they create, then the two
spelling conventions) rather than by the PDF's numbered sequence. Neither
document is redistributed with this repository.

